\title{MCP Streamable HTTP Client}
\description{
Connect to a remote MCP server over the Streamable HTTP transport
(MCP 2025-06-18): a single endpoint that answers JSON-RPC \code{POST}s directly.

This is the transport most public MCP servers expose today (GPSAdb, and most
hosted tool servers). It is \emph{not} the same as \link{McpSseClient}, which speaks the
legacy 2024-11-05 HTTP+SSE transport (a long-lived \code{GET} stream announcing a
separate \code{POST} endpoint).

\code{McpHttpClient} inherits \link{McpClient}, so \code{list_tools()}, \code{call_tool()},
\code{call_tool_data()}, \code{list_resources()}, \code{list_prompts()}, \code{get_prompt()} and
\code{as_sdk_tools()} behave exactly as they do for a local (stdio) server — only
the transport differs. There is no child process and therefore no \verb{$process}.

Server-initiated requests (\code{sampling/createMessage}, \code{roots/list}) are not
supported: the 2025-06-18 transport delivers those on the optional \code{GET} SSE
stream, which many servers (including those that answer \code{GET} with 405) do
not offer. A \code{sampling_handler} is therefore neither used nor advertised.
}
